!!!!!!!!urban env!!!!!!!!!
suein=1               !suein  INTEGER switch 0/1/2/3/4 for meteorological input: 0 uses normal meteorological input added with 2 columns: lai and traffic, but uses constant lai from morphology; 1 uses normal meteorological input added with 2 columns: lai and traffic; 2 uses normal met+lai+traffic but overwrites lai with readings from additional file 'in.lai'; 3 uses normal meteorological input, and uses constant lai from morphology ; 4 uses normal met but overwrites morphology lai with readings from additional file 'in.lai'
suech=0               !suech  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Carbon dioxide emission from Human respiration
suelh=0               !suelh  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Latent heat emission from Humans
suesh=0               !suesh  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Sensible heat emission from Humans
suect=0               !suect  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Co2 emission from Traffic
suelt=0               !suelt  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Latent heat emission from Traffic
suest=0               !suest  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Sensible heat emission from Traffic
suecb=0               !suecb  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Carbon emissions from Buildings heating
suelb=0               !suelb  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Latent heat emissions from Buildings heating
suese=0               !suese  INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Sensible heat emission from pro-capita domestic consumption of Electricity
sueftd=0               !sueftd INTEGER switch 0/1: Imposes use of Fake Traffic Data also if real traffic data are present
suedst=0               !suedst INTEGER switch 0/1: introduces Daylight Saving Time for fake traffic data
suetay=4               !suetay INTEGER switch 1/2/3/4: modulates Traffic Along Year; ; 1 uses a flat mean traffic during the year; 2 is flat with a traffic reduction of 20% in Jul and Aug; 3 is flat with 94% in Jan and 104% in Jun (Vancouver, Christen et al., 2011); 4 is the Helsinki 2008-2009 proxy, ranging from 74% in July to 111% in apr may sep oct (Jarvi et al 2012, fig.3a).
suerl=2.2             !suerl  REAL      km, average Road Length into the footprint. Christen 2011 has a tower height of 26m and long-term-averaged 80% flux isoplets are about 550m away; helsinki has a 31m tall tower, so 80% isoplet (thought as mean footprint dimensions) will be around 600m away; mean road lenght travelled by a vehicle will be roughly equal to footprint size (which in Helsinki is near the mainroad length into the footprint) plus a small fraction of addictional length in minor roads, so for Helsinki rl will be around 1.4 km. However, best results for Helsinki are obtained with suerl=2.2 km. This reflects uncertainties in traffic count, traffic composition, vehicle emission and footprint definition, as discussed in Jarvi 2012. Thus road length may be thought essentially as an adjustable parameter.
suehems=201.5           !suehems        REAL    umol_co2 s-1 person-1, human emission of co2 trough respiration, ref:Moriwaki and Kanda, 2004, used also by Christen et al 2010, 2011. Equivalent to 76.3 kg C year-1 person-1.
suehuc=1.0             !suehuc REAL      kg_h2o day-1 person-1, human production of water vapour, dayly amount of evaporated water from human metabolism (respiration, transpiration, sweat) and activities (combustion of cooking gas, evaporation from foods while cooking, domestic usage of hot/warm water, drying clothes to air, floor washing, plant watering, humidifiers), no ref
suevc=0.115           !suevc  REAL      lt km-1 vehicle-1, urban fuel consumption of a vehicle, ref: Christen 2010 referred to gasoline cars has .115
suedd=0.832           !suedd  REAL      kg lt-1, traffic fuel density, for diesel ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diesel_fuel has .832
suewpt=1.242           !suewpt REAL      kg_h2o kg_fuel-1, Water Produced by Traffic fuel combustion. For diesel, from ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diesel_fuel results that About r=86.1% of the fuel mass is carbon; being m_diesel=m_C+m_H, it is m_H=m_diesel*(1-r); being m_H=n_H*mw_H where n is the number, mw the molecular weight of the atom; from ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen being mw_H=1.008 g mol-1, n_H2o=n_H/2, mw_o=15.999 g mol-1 from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxygen, it results m_h2o/m_diesel=(1-r)*(2*mw_h+mw_o)/2*mw_h=1.242
suewpb=1.242           !suewpb REAL      kg_h2o kg_fuel-1, Water Produced by Building-heating fuel combustion. For diesel, from ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diesel_fuel results that About r=86.1% of the fuel mass is carbon; being m_diesel=m_C+m_H, it is m_H=m_diesel*(1-r); being m_H=n_H*mw_H where n is the number, mw the molecular weight of the atom; from ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen being mw_H=1.008 g mol-1, n_H2o=n_H/2, mw_o=15.999 g mol-1 from http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxygen, it results m_h2o/m_diesel=(1-r)*(2*mw_h+mw_o)/2*mw_h=1.242
diet=3500.           !diet           REAL      !kcal day-1 person-1, dayly calories in human diet. ref: FAO, WHO, cited in National Geographic Italia, november 2013, says that the average dayly availability of food in europe is between 3800 for austria and 2700 for moldavia. world average is 2800.
sueec=156.3           !sueec  REAL      W person-1, domestic electric consumption pro capita, ref: Istat for Sassari 2011 gives an equivalent 1369.4 kWh year-1 person-1, http://dati.istat.it/Index.aspx?DataSetCode=DCCV_CNSENRG
suehhp=47.3            !suehhp REAL      MJ/kg_fuel, Higher Heat Power of combustion of traffic fuel; for diesel 47.3, ref: http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potere_calorifico; see also Annamalai and Ishwar, 2006
suelhp=44.4            !suelhp REAL      MJ/kg_fuel, Lower Heat Power of combustion of building-heating fuel; for diesel 44.4, ref: http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potere_calorifico; see also Annamalai and Ishwar, 2006
suewth=0.25            !suewth REAL      m, thickness of each layer of which wall/roof are composed; the number of layers is equal to nsoil0 in input morphology. Original RDP value for suewth was 0.25m.
suehbf=0.5             !suehbf REAL      m3 m-3, [0,1] heated building fraction, heated volume of a building over total volume. It excludes non-heated volumes like external walls, stairs, lift vain, garages, and whole buildings used as stores, stations, and so on. No ref
suehep=30.             !suehep REAL      W m-3, HEating Power for unit volume for buildings, for Sassari 30, ref: http://www.stiliac.it/calor/it/area-tecnica/calcolo-fabbisogno-termico.html, http://www.kaldaie.com/dimensionamento-i-6.html, both referring to Italian DPR n.412 26/08/1993, published in Gazzetta Ufficiale n.242 14/10/1993, supplemento ordinario n.96, and subsequents integrations. Using this value to estimate fuel consumption in my house we have dc=600m3*6h*3600s/h / 44.4MJ/kg = 9kg of diesel in a cold winter day, wich matches with real consumption measured. So this value 30 W m-3 effectively represents the average power of domestic heating during the hours it is turned on (supposed 6 per day).
suecp=3.2523  !suecp  REAL      !kg_co2 kg_fuel-1, Carbon dioxide Produced by combustion of 1 kg of building-heating fuel. For diesel, from ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diesel_fuel is found that the CO2 emissions from diesel are 73.25 g/MJ; from http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potere_calorifico diesel offers a lower heating value of 44.4 MJ/kg , and a higher heating value of 47.3 MJ/Kg; so cp=73.25*1e-3*44.4 kg/kg=3.2523
sueve=285.            !sueve  REAL      !g_co2 km-1 vehicle-1, mean co2 Vehicle Emission, ref: Jarvi et al. (2012) finds typical co2 emission per vehicle class from "VTT: LIISA2010: Road traffic exhaust emissions calculation software (http://lipasto.vtt.fi/liisa), Technical Research Centre of Finland, VTT, 2011". Then a typical traffic distribution of 83% passenger cars, 10% vans, 3% trucks and 4% buses (Lilleberg and Hellman, 2011) was assumed, giving a mixed fleet CO2 emission factor of 285 g km−1. However, from EC data they infer an emission value of 380 g_co2 km-1. Also confirmed by http://carfueldata.dft.gov.uk .
suerai=0.121   !suerai REAL      !m2 m-2, Roof Area Index, total area occupied by buildings into the footprint, divided by the footprint area. This implicitly suppose flat roofs. For Sassari, with a square footprint of side 1.2km centered on EC tower, a value of 0.121 have been estimated. Frueh et al 2011 for Frankfurt give values variable from .02 and .68, a median value could be 0.3. From Burian et al morphological analysis 2002 (pag 24, 23 in pdf), we have rai .3 for Los Angeles. mexico city .47 (Grimmond and Oke 1999), vancouver .37 (Voogt and Oke 1997). If set < 0, uses original ACASA proxy: xrai proportional to popdens, cut off at 50%.
suewai=2.0             !suewai REAL      !m2 m-2, Wall Area Index, total area occupied by external walls (excluding roofs) of buildings (only those in direct contact with external air) into the footprint, divided by the footprint area. Frueh et al 2011 for Frankfurt give values variable from .9 and 29.25, a median value could be 3. From Burian et al 2002 morphological analysis,for los angeles one can estimate wai=lambdac-rai=1.51-.3=1.21 (page 43, 44 in pdf). If set < 0, uses original ACASA proxy: xwai proportional to popdens^3.
suebh1=22.             !suebh1 REAL      !--, [0,24] hour after which all Building Heatings are supposed turned off. For Sassari a meaningful value could be 22. If you want building heating turned on everytime, set this to 24.
suebh2=8.              !suebh2 REAL      !--, [0,24] hour before which all Building Heatings are supposed turned off. For Sassari a meaningful value could be 8. If you want building heating turned on everytime, set this to 0.
suetol=10.             !suetol REAL      !K, temperature tolerance before turning on heating. If external temperature is lower than the chosen tendo (thermostatic temperature inside buildings) but within the tolerance, don't turn on heating. Original ACASA value was 10K. Note that at the moment, if external temperature is higher than tendo, air conditioning is never invoked.
!!!!!!!!!!!end urban!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!

