!
! ACASA is an ecophysiological process-based numerical simulation algorithm intended for use in the STEM arena, namely research/education 
! (teaching), and the praxes of public and environmental health. it is imbued with both late 20th-centuray language and logic structures 
! commonly found in the atmospheric sciences, particularly biometoeology (biomicrometeorology).
!
! one of its product sets is its estimatations of the total exchange of energy, mass, and momentum (heat, water vapor, CO2, momentum) 
! between the Earth's surface and lower atmosphere (lower PBL) under any and all meteorological and surface-morphological circumstances 
! of a finite measure, including Earth-actual and reasonably imagined climate and land-use scenarios.  a single "call" to ACASA represents 
! a particular scale (which are aggregates of micropixel/subscale--"subcanopy" or "leaf-scale"--exchanges), which then can be aggregated
! (like pixels) to represent larger scales in space and/or time as conditions vary. given 
! this is an insitu version, it is designed to run for successtive 30-minute periods for one geographical point. with this model, one can 
! resolve the diurnal cycle of microenvironemntal conditions and and flux-exchanges at "convective time-scale" resolutions.
!
! the scale of application is of the "EC tower flux footprint" or "Reynold's averaging" regime: ~30 minutes, ~1 km2 horizontal 
! by ~100 m vertical, for most ecosystems. these scales of application ! may vary by a factor of 2 or so but not quite a factor of 10.
! 
! additional ACASA work includes: <additional cite List / bibliography goes here>
!
! indirectly and directly, this work has been borne from the scientific community at large, and because of this it is not only 
! of the public domain, it is also a public asset whose value has yet to be ascertained. 
!
! proprietary concerns over the management of ACASA code development arise from its sheer complexity. 
! it ordinarily takes a person years to get familiar with the code. 
!
! suggested restrictions on code development include having a central site at the corresponding institutions 
! where the code is maintained and where all updates are collated and documented...much like what exists for WRF at NCAR. currently, 
! this location is at UC Davis <website TBA> with a mirror site at U. Sassari <also TBA>.
!
! the suggestion that UC Davis / U Sassari remain the central development hub for ACASA, which includes letting Pyles continue to develop
! the ACASA code, exists only to reduce any negative impacts arising from errors that may remain both in the code and in its usage/application. 
! warning: the learning curve required to understand *all* of the code is not trivial. caveat usitur: be wary of inheriting other peoples' 
! deadlines when working with this model, and establish your own deadlines appropriately.
!
! for the past forty years, computational algorithm intelligence has proceeded roughly apace with observed increases in human cognitive 
! abilities. data storage & retrieval, along with calculation speed, have on the other hand, increased millions-fold over this time. 
! hence the bottleneck that inhabits the realm between what we want to have happen and the tools we have to make it happen.
! this is not only true for this model, it also appears true for science in general, as well as politics and religion. 
!
! eventually, the realms of ACASA application will extend beyond comparing observed and modeled fluxes, beyond numerical weather forecasting,
! beyond carbon-footprint assessments, and will include more efforts in the realm of ecosystem repair, regeneration, and thrival. 
! in the future, such a model could be useful in creating and/or maintaining Terran ecosystems and climates in space and/or other planets.
!
! RDP December 2012 -  / General: check for inconsistencies in H, LE for cases of canopy standing water, etc.
!
! 0) made sure all summations of flux source components (heat, momentum, h2o,co2) occur correctly. improved (corrected) the intended 
! logic for calculation of dry/wet/iced areal fractions of each canopy element-types (xlint(i,j,k)) in subroutine intercept.
! the current version of the model is now much more sensitive to the presence of standing water/ice within the canopy than previously.
! THE IMPACT OF THIS IS TO ALLOW FOR A BROADER ARRAY OF SPATIOTEMPORAL VARIATIONS IN LE (<W'Q'> THAN INDICATED  PRIOR TO THIS CORRECTION.
! it has yet to be determined if and how this improves the flux calculations overall. 
! this is also the last version to emerge from "the wild", for as of now, we will be installing version control protocols
! for handing future debugging and/or modifications to model physics.
!!...

